AB  - The purpose of this descriptive correlational study was to evaluate the quality of life, coping behaviors and related factors in women with nocturia. One hundred and ninety-nine women with nocturia, who were members of staff at a medical center in central Taiwan, were recruited by purposive sampling. A structured questionnaire was used to collect data from them via self-administration. The results were that: (1) The mean score for quality of life was 82.61 (SD = 11.5). (2) The most frequently reported problems were worry about not getting enough sleep at night, taking care about when and how much to drink, and worry that the condition might deteriorate. (3) The most effective coping behaviors were reducing tea or coffee intake, drinking less after dinner, and performing pelvic muscle exercise. (4) Multivariate regression and logistic regression analysis indicated that subjects with better quality of sleep, less episodes of nocturnal void, and employment in non-manual labor had a better quality of life. The subjects who percived nocturia as an illness and had a lower quality of life engaged in more treatment-seeking behavior. The results of this study may help medical personnel understand the quality of life and coping behaviors of women with nocturia. They may also serve as a reference in the provision of better care for women with nocturia.
